<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Muette de Portici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1828</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as The Mute Girl of Portici, Masaniello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Muette de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portici">Portici</a> is a French grand opera by Daniel Auber, first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1828. Set during the Neapolitan revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaniello">Masaniello</a> against Spanish rule in 1647, it includes the patriotic duet &quot;Amour sacré de la patrie&quot;. A performance at the Théâtre de la Monnaie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 25 August 1830 sparked the riots that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-muette-de-portici.jpg" alt="La Muette de Portici" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: film screenshot (Universal Film Manufacturing Company) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anna_Pavlova_in_The_Dumb_Girl_of_Portici.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
